INT. VINEYARD TASTING ROOM - AFTERNOON

Golden light through tall windows. Rows of oak barrels line the back wall. A long bar of reclaimed wood, a few empty glasses, a bucket of ice.

CLAUDETTE ARMSTRONG-BEAUMONT, 30s, in a linen shirt, wipes down the bar. She hums. She's happy to be alone.

The door chimes. In steps GREGOR VANDERWAAL, 30s, sunglasses on his head, a messenger bag across his chest. He pauses on the threshold, as if he has forgotten what he came for.

                    CLAUDETTE
We're closed.

                    GREGOR
I know.

                    CLAUDETTE
The sign says closed.

                    GREGOR
I read the sign.

                    CLAUDETTE
And?

                    GREGOR
I wanted to say something to you.

She keeps wiping. Slower now. Her back stiffens, and her eyes find his reflection in the glass behind the bar.

                    CLAUDETTE
Then say it.

                    GREGOR
Can I sit?

                    CLAUDETTE
Suit yourself.

He sits on a stool two down from where she works. Sets down his bag. Straightens it. Straightens it again.

                    GREGOR
I've rehearsed this for eleven months.

                    CLAUDETTE
Then it should be perfect.

                    GREGOR
It isn't.

                    CLAUDETTE
Go on.

                    GREGOR
When you left, I told everyone it was mutual.

                    CLAUDETTE
It was.

                    GREGOR
It wasn't.

                    CLAUDETTE
I said it was.

                    GREGOR
You said it because I couldn't.

She folds the rag. Lays it flat. Stares at it.

                    CLAUDETTE
I got a job in Napa. I took it.

                    GREGOR
I know.

                    CLAUDETTE
You could have asked me to stay.

                    GREGOR
I was afraid you'd say yes.

                    CLAUDETTE
That's absurd.

                    GREGOR
I know.

                    CLAUDETTE
What were you afraid of?

                    GREGOR
That you'd stay and resent me.

                    CLAUDETTE
I wouldn't have.

                    GREGOR
You would have, a little.

She almost smiles. Catches it. Turns to the barrels, hands flat on the counter, her back to him.

                    CLAUDETTE
It's a good vintage this year.

                    GREGOR
I'm sure it is.

                    CLAUDETTE
I made the blend myself.

                    GREGOR
Of course you did.

                    CLAUDETTE
Do you want to taste it?

A long beat. He doesn't answer. She turns. Takes down a glass, pours a splash of dark red, and slides it across the bar.

He lifts it. Smells. Tastes. His eyes close.

                    GREGOR
Plums.

                    CLAUDETTE
And?

                    GREGOR
Something smoky.

                    CLAUDETTE
Cedar. From the barrel.

                    GREGOR
It's wonderful.

                    CLAUDETTE
It's slightly overworked.

                    GREGOR
Don't undersell it.

                    CLAUDETTE
Don't flatter me.

                    GREGOR
Claude.

                    CLAUDETTE
Don't call me that.

                    GREGOR
Claudette.

                    CLAUDETTE
Yes.

                    GREGOR
Did you see my letters?

                    CLAUDETTE
I saw them.

                    GREGOR
Did you read them?

She turns away again. Reaches under the bar. Brings out a shoebox, wrapped in twine, its lid curled from handling. She sets it between them.

He stares at it. She stares at him. Neither moves.

                    CLAUDETTE (CONT'D)
All of them.

His hand reaches out. Covers hers. The box sits beneath their joined fingers.

The door chimes again behind them. A delivery man in a brown uniform sticks his head in, sees the pair of them frozen, and quietly withdraws.
